<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Władysław the White</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1327–1388</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Wladyslaw the White of Gniewkowo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Władysław the White was the last Duke of Gniewkowo, of the Kuyavian branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>. In 1363 he sold his duchy to King Casimir III and left on pilgrimage, later becoming a monk. After Casimir's death in 1370 he claimed the Polish throne and fought for Gniewkowo against Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, but in 1377 he gave up his claims for compensation and spent his last years in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-the-white.jpg" alt="Władysław the White" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous plate · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_Bia%C5%82y_seal_1355.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
